down-new-styling"><p data-start=3D"0" data-end=3D"356">Ah! This is a critic=
al question =E2=80=94 designing caching <strong data-start=3D"52" data-end=
=3D"106">for a workload dominated by dynamic Specifications</strong> is ver=
y different from a simple entity L2 cache. Since most of your queries are d=
ynamic, the naive approach of caching entities or query results is almost a=
lways <strong data-start=3D"269" data-end=3D"293">ineffective or risky</str=
ong>. Here=E2=80=99s a structured approach for a production-ready design.</=
p>
<hr data-start=3D"358" data-end=3D"361">
<h1 data-start=3D"363" data-end=3D"421"><strong data-start=3D"365" data-end=
=3D"421">1=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3 Principles for Caching with Heavy Specificatio=
ns</strong></h1>
<ol data-start=3D"423" data-end=3D"1426">
<li data-start=3D"423" data-end=3D"732">
<p data-start=3D"426" data-end=3D"495"><strong data-start=3D"426" data-end=
=3D"493">Cache the immutable / reference data, not dynamic query results</s=
trong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"499" data-end=3D"732">
<li data-start=3D"499" data-end=3D"609">
<p data-start=3D"501" data-end=3D"609">L2 cache and Spring cache shine for =
<strong data-start=3D"537" data-end=3D"562">single-entity lookups</strong> =
or <strong data-start=3D"566" data-end=3D"606">small, rarely changing looku=
p tables</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"613" data-end=3D"732">
<p data-start=3D"615" data-end=3D"732">Dynamic Specification queries genera=
te unpredictable SQL =E2=86=92 low cache hit ratio, high invalidation =E2=
=86=92 <strong data-start=3D"715" data-end=3D"731">cache thrash</strong>.</=
p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"734" data-end=3D"1026">
<p data-start=3D"737" data-end=3D"798"><strong data-start=3D"737" data-end=
=3D"796">Separate =E2=80=9Creference data=E2=80=9D vs =E2=80=9Ccomputed / q=
uery results=E2=80=9D</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"802" data-end=3D"1026">
<li data-start=3D"802" data-end=3D"945">
<p data-start=3D"804" data-end=3D"945">Reference data: countries, statuses,=
 roles, etc. =E2=86=92 cache safely with Spring <code data-start=3D"880" da=
ta-end=3D"892">@Cacheable</code> or distributed cache (Caffeine, Redis, Haz=
elcast).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"949" data-end=3D"1026">
<p data-start=3D"951" data-end=3D"1026">Query results: either dynamically c=
omputed on-demand or materialized in DB.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1028" data-end=3D"1237">
<p data-start=3D"1031" data-end=3D"1072"><strong data-start=3D"1031" data-e=
nd=3D"1070">Avoid caching entity lists directly</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"1076" data-end=3D"1237">
<li data-start=3D"1076" data-end=3D"1143">
<p data-start=3D"1078" data-end=3D"1143">Entity graphs are mutable =E2=86=
=92 caching them risks <strong data-start=3D"1125" data-end=3D"1140">stale =
reads</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1147" data-end=3D"1237">
<p data-start=3D"1149" data-end=3D"1237">If you must cache a dynamic query =
result, cache <strong data-start=3D"1197" data-end=3D"1220">immutable DTOs =
only</strong>, never entities.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1239" data-end=3D"1426">
<p data-start=3D"1242" data-end=3D"1292"><strong data-start=3D"1242" data-e=
nd=3D"1290">Leverage DB / query-level optimization first</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"1296" data-end=3D"1426">
<li data-start=3D"1296" data-end=3D"1426">
<p data-start=3D"1298" data-end=3D"1426">Indexes, partial indexes, material=
ized views, and query projection usually give <strong data-start=3D"1378" d=
ata-end=3D"1396">far better ROI</strong> than caching dynamic results.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
